# verify/compose-4 — independent verification of perf/shard-mapping + perf/blockshift-bpc

Date 2026-08-08. Host Apple M4 Pro (8P+4E, 12 logical), macOS 26.5.2, aarch64.
Default features. No `-C target-cpu=native`. No `nice` on any timed run.
Base: `main` @ 2aa00c5. Both branches under review: `fc120a8`.

Raw data in this directory:
  verify_compose4_gap_2026-08-08.tsv       160 rows, the dav1d sweep, foreign=0 on every row
  verify_compose4_cast_ab_2026-08-08.tsv    81 rows, base vs cast
  verify_compose4_rung_ab_2026-08-08.tsv    63 rows, the Part-1 falsification

Instruments: `scripts/perf/verify_gap.sh` (two-point wall fit, strict idle gate)
+ `scripts/perf/v4_ab.sh` / `v4_report.py` (in-process timer, paired per-round
ratios) + `scripts/perf/md5_setdiff.py` + `scripts/perf/bucket_selftime.py`.

---

## 1. THE FRAMING CORRECTION: both branches' Part 1 is dead on current main

Both branches were cut from `fc120a8`. `main` has since gained
**c5d1b00 (#458), `SHARDS_SERIAL = 1`**, which sets `mask = 0` on any instance
of a process that has declared no parallelism. Two consequences:

* `shard_of(b, 0) == 0` for every block, so no span can reach the wide path.
* `add`'s fast-path test is `(b0 != b1 && self.mask != 0) || state != 0`, so the
  **block shift is structurally unreachable at t=1** — the only thing it feeds
  is `b0 != b1`, which is `&&`-ed with a false constant.

Measured, `examples/probe_tracker --features probe-wide`, 10 frames, on main:

```
  cell                  slow    multi   w_shards  w_blocks  w_full  wide_total
  v4k_8tile      t=1       0        0          0         0       0           0
  v4k_8tile_10b  t=1       0        0          0         0       0           0
  v4k_8tile      t=8   57551    57551          0         0       0           0
  v4k_8tile_10b  t=8   59855    59855          0         0       0           0
```

At `fc120a8` the shard-mapping agent measured 5,033,100 (8bpc) and 3,111,250
(10bpc) wide promotions per 10 frames in these cells. On main it is zero, and
`add_multi` is zero too. There is nothing left for a coarser serial block to
buy.

**Falsified by wall clock, not only by argument.** `blockshift-15` / `-16` are
main's own fixed rungs and set exactly the granularity both Part-1 mechanisms
compute at 4K. Paired per-round ratios vs main, n=7, md5-identical, t=1:

```
  arm       v4k_8tile (8bpc)              v4k_8tile_10b (10bpc)
  shift 15  0.9960 [0.9716, 1.0071]       0.9973 [0.9897, 1.0096]
  shift 16  1.0089 [0.9954, 1.0105]       1.0007 [0.9912, 1.0209]
```

Every band straddles 1.000. At `fc120a8` the same rungs measured 0.936 / 0.937
at 8bpc and 1.049 / 1.044 at 10bpc. **Both the −5.6% win AND the +4.9% 10bpc
regression were artifacts of the pre-#458 wide path**, and both are gone.

Therefore: shard-mapping's `SERIAL_RUN` ladder, and blockshift-bpc's
`set_pixel_bytes` latch + serial adaptive shift, are **not composed**. The
latter would have added a process-global monotone latch, a public API function
and a call in `decode.rs` purely to gate a mechanism that now measures null in
both directions.

Neither branch is at fault: both measured their own base correctly. The
mechanism was overtaken.

## 2. WHAT IS COMPOSED: blockshift-bpc Part 2 (the zerocopy cast), and only that

`cast_slice_failed` (`#[cold] #[inline(never)]`) replaces `.unwrap()` on
`mut_from_bytes`/`ref_from_bytes` — the predicate is untouched, zerocopy still
decides — plus `#[inline(always)]` on `slice_as`/`mut_slice_as`. This is
independent of the tracker and survives #458.

Live on main before the change: six out-of-line
`slice_as`/`mut_slice_as::<_, u16>` symbols in the binary. After: zero.

Paired per-round ratios vs main, n=9, in-process timer, md5-IDENTICAL every row:

```
  cell                        ratio     [min      max]     base_ms   head_ms
  v4k_8tile_10b  t=1         0.9380   0.9261   0.9517      447.83    419.47
  v4k_8tile_10b  t=8         0.9351   0.8632   0.9507       87.36     82.16
  v4k_8tile      t=1  (ctl)  1.0043   0.9940   1.0121      368.21    369.23
```

The 8bpc cell is the instrument's calibration: at one byte per pixel `V == u8`
and the whole cast folds away, so a null there is what a correct measurement
looks like. It straddles 1.000.

## 3. Gap to dav1d — same session, same interleaved sweep, idle box

`verify_gap.sh`, 4 arms interleaved with rotating order, two-point fit
(2 and 20 frames) so process startup drops out, median of n=5 rounds. The
strict idle gate discarded 2 cells and **every committed row has foreign=0**.
dav1d 1.5.4 from Homebrew, measured at `--framedelay 1` (the tile-threading
model we implement) and at its shipping default.

ms/frame:

```
  vector           t     base     head   dav1d_def  dav1d_fd1
  v4k_8tile        1    365.5    364.9       246.5      246.5
  v4k_8tile        2    216.1    216.6       125.8      125.1
  v4k_8tile        4    115.7    116.3        63.9       65.7
  v4k_8tile        8     70.2     70.9        32.3       34.5
  v4k_8tile_10b    1    443.6    414.8       251.3      251.3
  v4k_8tile_10b    2    244.8    223.3       128.7      127.6
  v4k_8tile_10b    4    129.1    119.9        65.4       66.8
  v4k_8tile_10b    8     81.8     75.6        34.2       36.1
```

Ratio to `dav1d_fd1`:

```
  vector           t     base     head
  v4k_8tile        1     1.48     1.48
  v4k_8tile        2     1.73     1.73
  v4k_8tile        4     1.76     1.77
  v4k_8tile        8     2.04     2.05
  v4k_8tile_10b    1     1.77     1.65
  v4k_8tile_10b    2     1.92     1.75
  v4k_8tile_10b    4     1.93     1.80
  v4k_8tile_10b    8     2.26     2.09
```

The 10bpc column improves at all four thread counts; the 8bpc column does not
move at all. **The gap is 1.48x-2.09x and is >1.3x in every one of the eight
cells.** Thread scaling is unchanged (t=1->t=8: 5.15x head vs 7.14x dav1d_fd1),
so nothing here touched the scaling deficit.

## 4. Per-bucket profile — did the tracker share actually move?

`/usr/bin/sample`, 40 s, t=1, self-time leaves (`sample_selftime.py`), bucketed
by `bucket_selftime.py`. Shares converted to ms/frame using the §3 medians, so
the two arms are comparable despite head fitting more frames into the same 40 s.

```
  10bpc (v4k_8tile_10b, t=1)      base %   head %    base ms/f  head ms/f   delta
    entropy                        50.29    52.37        223.1      217.2    -5.9
    tracker                        23.79    19.84        105.5       82.3   -23.2
    kernels                        21.67    23.73         96.1       98.4    +2.3
    runtime                         2.47     2.17         11.0        9.0    -2.0
    TOTAL                                                443.6      414.8   -28.8

  8bpc (v4k_8tile, t=1) — control
    entropy                        60.64    60.19        221.6      219.6    -2.0
    tracker                        16.94    17.42         61.9       63.6    +1.7
    kernels                        18.71    18.98         68.4       69.3    +0.9
    TOTAL                                                365.5      364.9    -0.6
```

**Yes — the tracker bucket moved, and it is the whole story at 10bpc: −22.0%
(105.5 → 82.3 ms/frame), against a total of −28.8.** The named symbols confirm
the mechanism rather than merely correlating with it:

```
  base 10bpc  slice_as::<_,u16>      2.71%  + mut_slice_as 2.33% + 0.13% + 0.00%
              = 5.17% of self time   = 22.9 ms/frame
  head 10bpc  absent (inlined)       = 0
```

So 22.9 of the 28.8 ms is the cast wrapper itself; the remainder is knock-on
(runtime −2.0, entropy −5.9 from the caller's registers no longer being spilled
around the call). `add::<false>` / `add::<true>` are FLAT in absolute terms
(33.9 → 33.0 and 33.0 → 32.8 ms/frame) — this change does not pay for itself
out of the fast path, which is the distinguishing feature versus the Part-1
mechanism, where coarsening the block made `add` markedly more expensive.

At 8bpc every bucket moves less than 2 ms/frame and the total is −0.6 — null,
as the mechanism requires.

Note for the standing gap goal: the tracker is **17-24% of self time at t=1**,
and dav1d has no borrow tracker at all. That bucket, not the kernels, is where
the t=1 gap lives.

