## 6. THE SOUNDNESS FINDING: #458 silently made `wide_exclusion` vacuous

`wide_exclusion.rs` is the crate's only gate for the wide-path lock-ordering
TOCTOU. Its instance is built with no parallelism declared, so since
`SHARDS_SERIAL = 1` it gets `mask = 0` — **one shard — and never enters the wide
path at all.** Measured with a throwaway probe on unmodified main: 100
whole-buffer borrows of an 8 MiB instance produced 0 promotions on every
`wide_probe` counter. The file's two liveness assertions did not catch this
because they count GRANTED borrows, which a single-shard instance grants fine.

Consequence, measured: with hazard (a) planted (delete `add`'s in-lock `state`
re-read — the 4af62ae shape), on **unmodified main**:

```
  wide_exclusion    PASS (0.00s)   <- the designated gate
  soundness (25)    PASS
  shard_liveness(5) PASS
  narrow_release    PASS
```

**There was no gate at all for that hazard on main.**

Repair (this commit, test-only — the library binary is byte-identical, verified
by sha256): `wide_exclusion` now calls `set_parallelism` first, so the instance
gets the concurrent shard set that a real decode reaches the wide path through,
plus a `__probe_wide`-gated assertion that promotions actually occurred, so it
cannot go vacuous silently again. After the repair, with hazard (a) planted:

```
  wide_exclusion  FAIL, 1393 witnesses (--features __probe_wide)
  wide_exclusion  FAIL,  988 witnesses (plain)
```

restored -> green, source sha256 back to 900e165c…, `git diff` showing only the
intended hunks.

**Hazard (b), reported with its number and CONTRADICTING the handover.** The
shard-mapping report states that shortening `active()`'s prefix by one shard
fails this gate with 1 witness. Shortening it by one **passed 5 of 5 runs** here.
The gate does detect prefix holes — cutting the prefix TO one shard (the break
its own doc comment names) fails 3/3 with 9, 25 and 9 witnesses — but a
one-shard shortening is below its sensitivity. Treat the by-one result in that
report as at or past the edge of detectability, not as a demonstrated property.

## 7. Measured negatives, so nobody repeats them

* Serial block-shift coarsening at t=1 on main: **null at both depths**, §1.
  Do not rebuild a serial granularity policy without first re-checking the
  `probe-wide` counters — the mechanism it targets no longer exists.
* `set_pixel_bytes` / a stride or `BD::BPC` key for the block shift: not needed
  on main. It existed to dodge a 10bpc regression that is now null.
* Both branches were audited for the silent-deletion class (removed `#[inline]`
  / `#[inline(never)]` / assertions). **Clean this time**: blockshift-bpc's two
  removed `#[inline]`s are the ones upgraded to `#[inline(always)]`, and the
  removed assertions were replaced with 4-argument versions. shard-mapping
  replaced one gate assertion, though only under a non-default feature.
* shard-mapping's edit to `benchmarks/st1_kernels_2026-08-08.meta` marks its
  §4 item 2 "RESOLVED … −5.6% at 8bpc 4K". That claim does not hold on main
  (§1). It is not on main because the branch is not composed.

---

## 8. CORRECTION to section 2, and a contradicted handover claim

Section 2 and the first version of this branch's doc comment on `mut_slice_as`
carried "forcing the inline while that frame is still there ... measured 2.3-2.7%
SLOWER (verify/compose-4, n=11)". **That was the blockshift-bpc report's number,
measured on fc120a8, restated as if this campaign had produced it. It had not,
and it does not reproduce on main.** Cited-as-measured is exactly the failure
this campaign exists to catch, so it is corrected here rather than edited away.

Run properly: a 4-arm factorial, base / inline-only / cold-error-only / both,
paired per-round ratios vs main @ 2aa00c5, n=9, all md5-IDENTICAL, foreign=0.
Raw: `verify_compose4_factorial_2026-08-08.tsv`.

```
  cell                  inline-only              cold-only                both
  v4k_8tile_10b t=1  0.9862 [.9713,.9971]  0.9820 [.9684,.9962]  0.9374 [.9248,.9454]
  v4k_8tile_10b t=8  0.9760 [.9411,.9914]  0.9825 [.9588,1.0034] 0.9364 [.9136,.9486]
```

**Contradicted:** `#[inline(always)]` alone is NOT a regression on main. It is a
1.4% win at t=1 and 2.4% at t=8. The +2.3-2.7% the handover reports for that arm
does not appear at any thread count here.

**Upheld, and more strongly than the ordering story told it:** the two halves are
super-additive. Independent effects would predict 0.9862 x 0.9820 = 0.969; the
pair measures 0.937, roughly double the combined single-knob effect. And each
half alone is small enough to be dismissed — inline-only's band reaches 0.9971,
which all but touches 1.000. So the operative lesson stands with a different
mechanism behind it: this had to be measured as a factorial, not as two
one-knob A/Bs, or it would have been sized at ~1.5% and possibly dropped. The
attributed cause ("the inline drags the cold frame into every call site") is
NOT supported on main; what is supported is only that neither half pays off
without the other.
